## (c) Analysis of the concentration dependence of the CD spectra
The data can be fit to an empirically derived Hill coefficient, which examines the cooperativity of the association according to equations (l） and (2):
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{K_{\mathrm{H}}=[P_{\mathrm{mon}}]^{n}/[P_{n}],}\\ &{[P_{\mathrm{T}}]=[P_{\mathrm{mon}}]^{n}/(K_{\mathrm{H}}+[P_{\mathrm{mon}}]),}\end{array}
$$
in which $P_{\mathrm{mon}},P_{\mathrm{n}}$ and $P_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ are the concentrations of the monomer, nmer and total peptide, respectively, $n$ is the Hill coefficient, and $K_{\mathrm{H}}$ is a dissociation constant. Equation (2) is solved numerically for $[P_{\mathrm{{mon}}}]$ using the root function of Mlab (Civilized Software)， and inserted into equation (3):
$$
\left[\theta_{\mathrm{222}}\right]=\left[\theta_{n\mathrm{mer}}\right](f)=\left[\theta_{n\mathrm{mer}}\right](([P_{\mathrm{mon}}]^{n}/K_{\mathrm{H}})/P_{\mathrm{T}},
$$
in which $[\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{{nmer}}}]$ is the mean residue ellipticity of the associated form of the peptide and $f$ is the fraction of the peptide in the associated form. The parameters, $[\theta_{\mathrm{nmer}}],n$ and $K_{\mathrm{H}}$ are then determined for each peptide with the nonlinear least squares method using Mlab. This method of analysis assumes that the ellipticity of the monomer is zero; and was confirmed by allowing the ellipticity of the monomer to be an additional adjustable parameter, in which case the determined value was consistently within $3000\mathrm{deg}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{dmol}^{-1}$ of zero.
Alternatively the data could fit a monomerdimer-trimer equilibrium as described in equation (7) in Results. The mass balance equation is:
$$
\begin{array}{r l r}{\lefteqn{[P_{\mathrm{{T}}}]=\mathrm{{M}}+2\left[\mathrm{{D}}\right]+3\left[\mathrm{{T}}\right]}}\\ &{}&{={[\mathrm{{M}}]}+2\left[\mathrm{{M}}\right]^{2}/K_{1}+3\left[\mathrm{{M}}\right]^{3}/K_{1}K_{2}.}\end{array}
$$
The mean residue ellipticity at a given peptide concentration is given by:
$$
[\theta_{\bf222}]=(\mathrm{[M]}[\theta_{\bf u}]+2[\mathrm{D}][\theta_{\mathrm{D}}]+3[\mathrm{T}][\theta_{\bf T}])/[\mathrm{P_{T}}].
$$
in which the mean residue ellipticity of the dimer, $[\theta_{\mathrm{D}}]$ and the trimer, $[\theta_{\mathrm{D}}]$ , are assumed to be identical; and the ellipticity of the monomer, $[\theta_{\mathrm{m}}]$ ,is assumed to be zero. The parameters $[\theta_{\mathrm{D}}],[\theta_{\mathrm{T}}],[K_{1}]$ and $[K_{2}]$ are then determined for each peptide by the nonlinear least squares method using Mlab. In several calculations, the ellipticity of the monomer was included as an additional adjustable parameter; the determined value of this parameter in each case was consistently within $3000\mathrm{deg}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{dmol}^{-1}$ of zero.
The data were also analysed according to equations (8) and (9) in Results. With this approach a given peptide is chosen as a reference peptide, and values of $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ are calculated from the concentration dependence of its cD spectrum. The data for each additional peptide are then calculated using the mass balance equation:
$$
\begin{array}{r l r}{\lefteqn{[P_{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleT}}]=[\mathrm{\bfM}]+2[\mathrm{\bfD}]+3[\mathrm{\bfT}]}}\\ &{}&{=[\mathrm{\bfM}]+2[\mathrm{\bfM}]^{2}/K_{1}^{\prime}+3[\mathrm{\bfM}]^{3}/K_{1}^{\prime}K_{2}^{\prime},}\end{array}
$$
in which $K_{1}^{\prime}$ and $K_{2}^{\prime}$ are as defined in equations (8) and (9). Equation (6) is once again numerically solved for [M] and inserted into equation (5). Now, however, because the values of $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ are known, the only fitted parameters are $\Delta\Delta\mathrm{G}_{\alpha}$ and the ellipticities of the dimers and trimers (assumed to be equal). Coil-Ser was chosen because we had an extensive set of data for this peptide with values of $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ of $8.1\pm2.5$ and $5.0\pm1.3$ ,respectively. The values of $\Delta\Delta\mathrm{G}_{\alpha}$ were then calculated for each of the remaining peptides and were referenced to Gly.
## 3.RESULTS
## (a) Sedimentation equilibrium studies
We had adequate quantities of four peptides previously used in the helix propensity experiments (O'Neil &
DeGrado l990) to determine their aggregation states by sedimentation equilibrium (see table l). At neutral $\mathrm{pH}$ ， room temperature, with no denaturants and peptide concentrations ranging from $20{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ，the peptides sediment as single, homogeneous species with molecular masses consistent with a trimeric aggregation state. These data are consistent with earlier studies that showed the peptides self-assembled into highly stable helical aggregates that only dissociate in the presence of $4{-}7{\mathrm{~M~}}$ urea (O'Neil & DeGrado 1990).
The peptides coil- $\mathrm{V_{a}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L_{d}}$ and coil- $\mathrm{\cdotL_{a}\mathrm{-L_{d}}}$ also were also found to be trimeric under these conditions, and only the peptide $\mathrm{Asn}_{16}$ -coil- $\mathrm{V_{a}\mathrm{-}L_{d}w a s}$ found to form dimers. At an initial loading concentration of $\mathrm{~l~mu~}$ this peptide sedimented as a single species with a molecular mass close to that expected for a dimer (see table 1).
The trimeric association state observed for coil- $\mathrm{L_{a}{-}L_{d}}$ is in marked contrast with the dimeric state reported by Hodges and co-workers for the parent coiled-coil peptide (Lau et al. l984). We therefore prepared the parent coiled-coil peptide, TM-43, and determined its aggregation state by sedimentation equilibrium (see figure 3) at initial loading concentrations of $60~\upmu\mathrm{u}$ or $600~\upmu\mathrm{{M}},$ $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ or 2.5, and $0.1\mathrm{~u~KCl}$ or $1.1\mathrm{~M~KCl}$ .At $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ (either combination of peptide and salt concentration), the data clearly indicate that this peptide forms trimers (see table 2). At $\mathrm{pH}2.5$ ，the peptide forms higher-order aggregates that are likely tetramers. One potential explanation for the difference between these findings and earlier data reported by Hodges (Lau et al. l984; Zhu et al.1993) might be in the values used for the partial specific volume and density. Using the $\mathrm{H_{2}O-D_{2}O}$ difference method (Edelstein & Schachman l973),we determined the partial specific
